in the business market.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:32.810
So if you think about the
Fortune 500, for the first,

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:36.140
I don't know, eight decades
that the Fortune 500 existed,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.780
that list was almost
entirely static.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.820
The same companies were
on it year after year,

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.040
to the point where if a
company fell off of the list,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.030
it was news.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.400
We talked about it.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.960
Over the course of the last
15 years, 52%, more than half,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.550
of the Fortune 500s
dropped off the list.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.850
That is a staggering number,
and it's particularly

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.020
scary to the manufacturers.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.280
Because for the majority
of the 20th century,

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.000
that's who dominated that list.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.440
Yeah, there were
a few retailers,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.500
some energy companies, some
financial services companies.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.670
But for the most part,
it was the manufacturers

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.300
that were dominating that list.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.050
So we are seeing pressures,
as manufacturers,

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.990
as industrial companies,
coming from every direction

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:19.310
that you can imagine.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:22.610
How we respond to that is going
to dictate how we actually

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.390
survive in this new economy.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.420
So you can't talk about digital
without the word disruptor

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.240
coming reasonably close
in the same sentence.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.240
It follows pretty
closely after digital.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.160
But I'm going to
argue that what we

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.130
