00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:21.970
So this begs the
question, and this

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:25.570
is really the important
thing, how are they doing it?

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:28.840
If they're able to not
spend that much more money

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:31.420
and yet achieve these
fantastic results,

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:34.000
what are they doing differently
than the rest of us?

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.652
What are they doing differently
than their competitors?

00:07:36.652 --> 00:07:38.110
So this is what I
think we're going

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:40.750
to spend the rest of
the time talking about.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:43.373
Setting the stage
with statistics

00:07:43.373 --> 00:07:45.540
and what the industry is
doing and all the pressures

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.140
that we're having
is interesting,

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.170
but I think we all are
interested in understanding how

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:51.860
do we achieve these results.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:54.400
How do we approach these
problems in different ways,

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.080
tackle them in
different ways, so

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.910
that we can start seeing some
of those massive benefits that

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:00.910
are available to us if
we do things a little bit

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:02.670
differently.

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:04.700
So let's talk
about what we think

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.540
are the top four things
that you should be thinking

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:09.780
about doing differently.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:12.530
You should be thinking
about changing your mindset.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:15.060
So the first thing is-- and
it sounds really simple,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.010
but it's actually probably the
hardest part of all of this.

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:21.830
Envision the actual
business change that you're

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:23.270
trying to accomplish.

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:26.000
What do you want to be when
you grow up as a company?

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.930
What do you want to
be in your industry?

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:31.190
How are you going to change the
way that you're doing business?

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:35.330
So this could be anything from
moving from being a product

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.640
company to a services
company, changing

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:39.500
the way you think
about R&amp;D. Maybe

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:40.970
it's not product R&amp;D anymore.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:42.950
Maybe you're looking
at innovation,

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.170
how to build true
innovation programs inside

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:46.340
of your company.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:47.840
How do you actually
change the way

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.790
that you're thinking
about your business

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:52.970
so that you can design
technology to help

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.450
you achieve that change?

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:59.410
OK, so this is everybody's
favorite slide.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.940
I'm the only one that likes it,
but bear with me a little bit.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.300
So I also have to apologize.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.210
I have managed to
develop something

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:09.230
in my foot that might
be plantar fasciitis.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:11.800
So I may sit up and down a
little bit throughout this

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.430
if my foot starts killing me.

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:15.560
So bear with me a
little bit here.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.003
But we're going
to talk about-- it

00:09:17.003 --> 00:09:19.670
looks like most of the people in
this room are IT professionals.

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:21.340
I've already asked
that question.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.070
How do you actually, then,
respond to this, right?

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.130
So I focused a lot on
the business challenges,

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.540
what the business leaders
need to be thinking about.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:32.680
But as IT leaders, we
have an enormous role

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:34.310
to play in all of this.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:36.970
We have to modernize the
way we think about IT,

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.310
the way we think about
partnering with our business

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:40.210
partners.

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:44.620
So everybody is very familiar
with this concept on the left.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.750
Anyone who's been in IT
for any period of time

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.840
understands the traditional
flow of projects.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.410
How do you run an IT project?

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.880
Whether it's a waterfall
project or an agile project

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.350
is sort of immaterial
in this conversation.

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.420
It still starts with
a solution idea.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:09:59.490
You have a project.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:02.002
You get leadership approval
to go do that project.

00:10:02.002 --> 00:10:03.460
You probably do a
proof of concept.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:05.860
Let's prove the technology,
make sure it works.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.780
Let's roll out a point solution,
even an IoT point solution,

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.780
that could reap real benefits
once we put it into production.

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:15.430
You can get benefits this way.

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.340
You can get outcomes
from those projects,

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.830
but which you can't
get is transformation.

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.460
What you can't get is
real business change.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:25.900
So you've got to think about
how to widen the aperture,

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:28.450
let more ideas
through the process,

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:32.080
get more connected programs
and projects rolling.

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:33.400
Forget leadership approval.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:35.170
Get stakeholder consensus.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:37.873
Understand all the people
that touch your process,

00:10:37.873 --> 00:10:39.790
whether it's your suppliers
and your customers

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:41.332
and your manufacturers
and the people

00:10:41.332 --> 00:10:42.640
working on the shop floor.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.160
Get them involved in designing
what this new business

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:47.830
model looks like and what
the technology looks like.

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:49.970
And do proofs of value.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:51.820
We don't need to
prove the technology.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:55.300
Anybody who's doing POCs at this
point to prove the technology

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:56.530
is missing the point.

00:10:56.530 --> 00:10:58.210
Technology works, I promise.

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:01.750
What we need to see is does
it actually solve the business

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.400
problem that you're
trying to solve?

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.110
And you can't find
that out in a lab,

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:07.870
and you can't find that out
with just doing a data model.

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.930
You have to put it in production
in a pilot environment.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.840
Do it quickly, see your
results, get them back.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.200
And then you're able to have
this truly connected value

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:20.110
chain where you're understanding
that, as a manufacturer,

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:23.200
the decisions that I'm making
on the customer experience side

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.810
are influencing everything
all the way back to my supply

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:26.500
chain.

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.840
And all of that has to
have a digital thread.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:30.940
All of that has to have
digital connectivity that

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:32.720
brings all of that together.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:35.320
So we need to be thinking
about how to modernize IT.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.610
And then, finally--
actually, not finally.

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:41.120
Sorry.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:42.260
I have two more.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:43.470
My third point.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.740
There is really nothing
that consultants like more

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:47.660
than numbers, except roadmaps.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.180
We really, really,
really like roadmaps.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.880
So talking a little bit
about what a roadmap looks

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:55.910
like for IoT, for
digital and IoT.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:57.530
It is a little bit different.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:11:59.600
There are different
principles behind it,

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.130
but it's the same
general concept.

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:04.940
How you prioritize and how
you sequence your initiatives

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:08.210
probably has a greater impact
on your success or failure

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:09.930
than almost anything else.

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:12.410
And the reason is
what you're going

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:15.980
to look to do is manage these
projects, almost like a stock

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:17.060
portfolio.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:19.100
You're looking to get
individual returns,

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:22.590
but it's actually more important
that you get the total return.

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.470
So if you're looking
at five different point

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:27.160
solutions at different
places within your factory--

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:29.360
one is an asset
management solution

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.550
that sits on one of
your cutting machines

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.630
if you're a metals manufacturer.

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:36.890
Another one is automation
that helps you make sure

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:38.990
that you've got better quality.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:42.470
You've got video analytics
to measure worker safety.

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:44.660
Those are great
individual use cases.

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:46.970
When you connect those
together and sequence them

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:50.180
in the right way, you're
actually incrementally learning

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:52.370
from the data that you're
getting from the first,

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:54.703
and you're taking those
returns that you're getting,

00:12:54.703 --> 00:12:56.120
those cost savings
you're getting,

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:59.100
and you're reinvesting
it in the transformation.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.580
So how you sequence
these roadmaps

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:05.060
is absolutely critical,
because the goal is not

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:06.570
to get projects done.

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:09.410
The goal is to realize
value, to get to the point

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.173
where you've got
top line growth--

00:13:11.173 --> 00:13:12.590
all those things
at the top, where

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:16.500
we talk about new channels, new
markets, new business models,

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:19.130
new ways of getting
money in the door.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:21.350
And all those things
at the bottom--

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:23.390
increased operational
effectiveness,

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:26.430
reduce costs, all of
those bottom line savings.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:29.240
So if you focus on the value
realization and sequence

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.400
and prioritize in
the right way, you

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:35.840
will see the value coming
out of these programs.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.410
And then, finally, you
got to have an ecosystem.

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:40.430
Ecosystems are not new.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:42.880
This is a age-old concept.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:44.920
We all exist in an ecosystem.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:48.140
But there are, in my
opinion, some differences,

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:51.890
some new things about
a digital ecosystem.

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:56.980
So I'm an easy example
of Hitachi and Google.

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:58.690
We are two huge companies.

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:01.840
Hitachi is number 71 on
the global 2000 list.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:04.920
Google, Alphabet, is number 65.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:06.560
These are massive companies.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.160
They're top 100 biggest
companies in the world,

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.325
and we're used to
controlling our own destiny.

00:14:12.325 --> 00:14:14.450
This is what we've done
for the bulk of our career,

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:16.930
especially on the Hitachi
side, as a manufacturer,

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:18.830
traditional business.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:21.400
What we're seeing is
we can't be the center

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:22.870
of our own universe anymore.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:25.960
We can't expect
all of our partners

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:29.170
and all of our customers
to orbit around us.

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:31.750
Instead, we've got to pick
the customers at the center.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.360
This is not new, but it is
critically more important

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:35.560
than ever before.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.470
Customer centricity
is core to digital.

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:41.500
Put your customers at the
middle of that ecosystem.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:44.240
Enable that ecosystem
with technology,

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:48.130
whether it's analytics
or cloud or IT, IoT, all

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:49.390
of these components.

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:53.260
And figure out how you then
connect your entire ecosystem

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:54.010
together.

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:57.730
It's no longer just supplier,
partner, customer, company.

00:14:57.730 --> 00:14:59.590
That used to be the ecosystem.

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:02.500
Now it is, how do you interact
with your governments,

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:05.490
with your communities, with
all of the world around you?

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:07.420
Because all of that
is required to make

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:09.650
yourself successful in digital.

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:12.605
So we're really excited about
our partnership with Google,

00:15:12.605 --> 00:15:14.980
but we also know, and I'm sure
my Google colleagues would

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:18.590
agree with me, even our two huge
companies can't do this alone.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:22.120
We have to have other partners
in the mix, other building

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:22.690
blocks.

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:25.000
All these things come
together to build

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:29.020
these digital solutions that
help drive transformation.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:31.840
So with that, I am
going to turn it over

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:34.280
to the guy who's
smarter than I am who's

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:36.640
going to talk about the
technical building blocks,

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.830
how you bring these
things together.

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:40.720
Unfortunately, you're
not done with me yet.

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:43.180
I'll be coming back up
on the stage when Rob

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:44.650
is done, if he ever finishes.

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:46.270
We'll see how that goes.

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:47.810
When Rob is done,
and we're going

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:50.670
to talk about case studies,
real-world examples,

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:53.320
the stuff you guys actually care
about-- what other companies

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:54.670
are doing in this space.

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:55.460
So I'll be back.

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:56.380
We'll talk in a bit.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:58.760
Thanks, guys.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:01.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:07.062
ROBERT LIVELY: Hello, everyone.

00:16:07.062 --> 00:16:08.395
How are we doing this afternoon?

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:14.850
OK, I'm done.

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:16.610
So, I'm really
excited to talk to you

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:21.050
guys about how we use
building blocks and solutions

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:24.350
within IoT, and industrial
IoT in particular,

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.195
to solve business
problems for our clients.

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:32.840
Now, something I think is
important to differentiate

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:38.030
as we start this is consumer
IoT versus industrial IoT.

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:40.550
They do share something
very important in common,

00:16:40.550 --> 00:16:43.850
and that is we are trying
to acquire data, bring

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:46.830
it someplace that we can do
something interesting with it.

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:52.670
However, in industrial IoT,
we see divergences in criteria

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:55.370
that we need to
satisfy that don't

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:57.955
exist within consumer IoT.

00:16:57.955 --> 00:17:00.080
Consumer IoT would be best
represented by something

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:03.110
like the Nest thermostat
that's on your wall.

00:17:03.110 --> 00:17:06.589
It's sending, essentially,
the same data up to the cloud

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:10.970
all the time from every one
of those Nest thermostats.

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:12.980
And it's using the
same kind of protocols,

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:14.773
and it's doing this directly.

00:17:14.773 --> 00:17:16.190
There is nothing
that mediates it.

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:18.619
It is directly connected
to the internet and sharing

00:17:18.619 --> 00:17:21.109
information on the cloud.

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:26.280
In industrial internet, if we
look at security as an example,

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:30.340
we don't connect these devices
directly to the internet.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:32.370
Even if they could,
it would be unwise,

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:35.670
because there's not a lot
of security on that existing

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:38.290
operational technology
infrastructure.

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:41.760
So we mediate it by putting
gateways in the middle.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:46.950
And in that case, the security
requirement becomes elevated.

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.200
Where we had security
on the consumer side,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.290
in the industrial side, we
need hardware routes of trust

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:54.810
so that these devices can
attest to the security

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:58.360
of their operating
system and the firmware.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:00.850
On the scalability
side, on consumer IoT,

00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:03.470
maybe you talk about millions
of devices connected up

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:07.390
and sending information,
all these Nest thermostats.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:09.340
On the industrial
side, we have to talk

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:13.810
to thousands of
devices and thousands

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:15.680
of different types of devices.

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:18.520
So we need to be able to
mediate that conversation

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.320
and have all sorts
of gateways that

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:22.990
are doing different
things at different times

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:24.650
with different purposes.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:27.370
So in order to
manage that, we need

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:28.900
to be able to do
things that allow

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:31.300
for a variety of
connectivity types

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:34.143
in a variety of different
devices that we need talk to.

00:18:34.143 --> 00:18:35.560
Because we'll talk
to sensors that

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:37.300
may provide
temperature information

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:40.390
using analog signals,
or we'll talk

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:44.080
to SCADA systems that have their
proprietary protocols or energy

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:47.870
sub-meters that speak Modbus,
but a particular dialect.

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:51.050
So we need to be able
to adapt to that.

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:52.550
But there are also
other things that

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:55.550
come up where they aren't
a part of consumer IoT.

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:57.140
We need to manage
performance, so we

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:59.690
have to be worried about
latency versus throughput

00:18:59.690 --> 00:19:01.620
in the data that comes through.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:04.970
I need to worry about do I
need guaranteed data delivery,

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:06.730
or I'm OK with loss tolerance.

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:09.420
And it's based on use cases.

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:14.060
So those kind of capabilities
don't necessarily

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:18.368
arise when we're talking about
consumer IoT applications.

00:19:18.368 --> 00:19:20.160
But fundamentally, the
most important thing

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:21.110
is at the bottom.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:23.710
Its solution enablement.

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:26.860
With industrial IoT, we're
trying to go and solve

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:28.940
problems for our customers.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:31.150
So the industrial
IoT application

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:35.170
needs to support the development
of flexible solutions

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:38.148
and modular deployment so we
can go and build something

00:19:38.148 --> 00:19:39.190
interesting on top of it.

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:45.270
So the challenge
here, though, is

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:49.570
Ellen talked about an ecosystem
of contributors, partners.

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:51.130
And we all live
in this ecosystem

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:53.860
and need services and components
from the different partners

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:55.820
that play in this.

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.120
But the ecosystem
also needs to support

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:02.210
the easy participation
of different vendors

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:04.490
in this environment.

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:08.840
Because plants and
manufacturing environments

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:10.360
are not greenfield.

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:14.270
When we go to deploy
IoT, we have, certainly,

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:17.450
core IoT services that
are new or providing

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:19.790
certain fundamental
capabilities to enable

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:25.730
us to bring on new devices
on the edge and in the fog.

00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:29.090
But we also find
that IoT touches

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:33.200
a wide range of
enterprise architecture

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:36.350
that provides some of these
fundamental capabilities we

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:38.570
were talking about
on the prior slide.

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:40.250
All that functional
requirement become

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:43.730
building block capabilities that
need to exist in order for us

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:45.780
to deploy solutions.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:47.540
So if I'm looking
at this, I can say,

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:50.180
sure, there's already
security in place,

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:53.240
mechanisms to provide secure
connectivity of devices

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:55.420
onto the network.

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:59.240
IoT needs this, but when
we go to a deployment,

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:01.710
we need to see what
already exists there

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:05.330
and if we need to amplify
or enhance that capability

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:08.700
or if we need to stand it
up because it doesn't exist.

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:12.110
So we need building blocks that
can do those different kinds

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:14.420
of capabilities for us.

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:17.660
And on the data side,
maybe the client

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:20.900
already has existing
streaming infrastructure.

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:22.430
We need to integrate with that.

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:25.550
We need to get it to provide
a common interface that we

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:28.613
are expecting it to provide
so that we can surface data

00:21:28.613 --> 00:21:30.280
up and do something
interesting with it.

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:36.090
This is where a strategic
partner like Google

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:38.040
comes into play.

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:40.800
Google provides building
blocks that align

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:43.110
to each of these
functional capabilities

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:46.080
are important for
industrial IoT.

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:48.810
And what's more, they
provide a variety

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:51.210
of building blocks in
these different areas.

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:54.660
So that as the need arises, I
can find the right tool set,

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:56.490
the right building
block to put in place

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:59.310
that enhances the existing
infrastructure that already

00:21:59.310 --> 00:22:01.260
exists in that environment.

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:05.070
Or I can stand up a
full-fledged environment,

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:08.010
full-fledged capability
when that capability doesn't

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:11.590
exist where I go to deploy.

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:16.080
And Google also is consistently
innovating and creating

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:16.960
new solutions.

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:19.800
So they're building new
capabilities, new solution

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:24.270
building blocks that I
can leverage and think

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:27.300
about new kinds of creativity,
new kinds of solutions

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:28.470
that didn't exist before.

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:35.000
So this modular approach
using building blocks

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:39.805
allows us to reduce the time it
takes to get to business value.

00:22:39.805 --> 00:22:41.180
So there was a
great presentation

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:45.140
by a gentleman Sam
Ghods at Box, where

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:48.140
he said a platform abstracts
away a messy problem

00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:50.620
so you can build on top of it.

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:52.790
And that's what the
building blocks are doing.

00:22:52.790 --> 00:22:56.570
They're creating a consistent
set of functionality

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:58.790
and a consistent way
the data is provided

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:01.340
and made available
through the infrastructure

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:03.800
so that we can focus on
building the solutions that

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:06.490
take advantage of that.

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:08.680
Again, Google is
innovating and providing

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:12.260
tools, such as Anthos that
they discussed the other day.

00:23:12.260 --> 00:23:15.110
In there, Thomas Kurian
talked about Anthos

00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:17.450
being a single platform
where you need it.

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:20.060
Because we want to
reduce risk, and we

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:22.660
want to reduce the strain on
the solution developers so

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:25.410
they can develop against
a common platform.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:29.530
And this is reducing our
friction to implementation.

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:31.860
So when we talk about
solutions, though, we

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:36.960
start talking about recipes,
and recipes have ingredients.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:39.060
In the solution,
the specific code

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:43.570
that runs on these building
blocks is an ingredient.

00:23:43.570 --> 00:23:46.330
The way we configure
those building blocks

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:48.590
is an ingredient.

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:50.300
And these ingredients
are technically

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:55.040
decoupled from those specific
building block [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:57.140
and are reusable.

00:23:57.140 --> 00:24:01.250
The building blocks-- if we
went with the recipe metaphor--

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:05.300
are things like your oven,
your stove, your pots and pans,

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:07.850
the things that help you
to accomplish what you need

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:11.140
to, to make the recipe work.

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:13.030
So let's take a very
specific example

00:24:13.030 --> 00:24:15.323
to make this a
little more concrete.

00:24:15.323 --> 00:24:16.990
So if we're doing
predictive maintenance

00:24:16.990 --> 00:24:21.920
on weld robots in an automotive
manufacturing environment,

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:26.390
I need to be able to handle
a couple of different things

00:24:26.390 --> 00:24:28.910
that need to deploy across
this entire infrastructure that

00:24:28.910 --> 00:24:31.673
allows me to go do
predictive maintenance.

00:24:31.673 --> 00:24:32.840
So what does that look like?

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:38.450
First ingredient, I
need the right data.

00:24:38.450 --> 00:24:41.900
So I can define what data
points need to be pulled out

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:43.230
of the environment.

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.860
And that becomes one of the
ingredients for my recipe.

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:48.050
That helps me to define
what sensors I need,

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:50.720
what kind of capabilities
need to be deployed

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:52.410
within that gateway device.

00:24:52.410 --> 00:24:54.410
Then, I can start talking
about the modules that

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:58.100
might need to exist in that
gateway that can talk to all

00:24:58.100 --> 00:25:01.580
of these components that
are providing me data

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:08.850
and what modules might need to
be there to transform the data.

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:13.130
I can talk about the AI machine
learning ingredient that

00:25:13.130 --> 00:25:16.310
needs to sit up in the cloud
that helps me to convert all

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:19.640
of this data, along with data
from the maintenance systems,

00:25:19.640 --> 00:25:22.370
into a predictive
maintenance model

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:25.400
that I can then use to
evaluate whether or not

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:28.863
something is about to go wrong
with that particular machine.

00:25:28.863 --> 00:25:30.280
So all of these
ingredients, then,

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:34.010
can be specified within
our solution recipe.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:37.090
And that's how we take
advantage of that building block

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:38.420
infrastructure.

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:42.490
So we are layering these
things and abstracting

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:44.630
the baseline
infrastructure that exists

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:47.780
in sensors and equipment,
the building blocks that

00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:51.500
take advantage of acquiring
that data and moving it around,

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:54.050
and then the solutions
that sit on top of that

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:57.680
and expect a common platform
and a common set of behaviors

00:25:57.680 --> 00:26:00.650
so that we can take a predictive
maintenance solution that we

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:03.470
already solved and move
it somewhere else, even

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:05.150
if the infrastructure
is different

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:07.130
and the set of components
and building blocks

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:10.310
that we deploy
there are different.

00:26:10.310 --> 00:26:13.070
So I'm going to dive one
section deeper and look

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.890
at what this would
look like on the edge

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:18.080
and give specific examples.

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:21.290
So on the edge,
the building blocks

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:24.110
are things like a common
runtime environment,

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:28.400
using containers, being able to
use a common message bus that

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.890
allows different containers
and modules that are running

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:33.020
on the edge to talk
with one another

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:37.430
in a consistent, uniform
specified fashion.

00:26:37.430 --> 00:26:40.420
It's the way we connect
backup to our control plane

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:42.800
so we can specify how
these edge devices are

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:47.530
managed through edge lifecycle
management capabilities.

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:48.900
What's the recipe?

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:50.490
The recipe is the
way I configure

00:26:50.490 --> 00:26:53.220
one of these connector modules
to talk to the device it needs

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:54.042
to speak to.

00:26:54.042 --> 00:26:55.500
So I have a connector
module that's

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:56.970
talking to that
temperature sensor.

00:26:56.970 --> 00:26:59.300
So it needs to know how
to read analog signals off

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:04.050
of an RS485 port, convert
that into data that I can use.

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:07.710
Or it's a Modbus module
that speaks to a sub-meter,

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:11.220
and I send parameters to
that Modbus module that say,

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:13.170
these are the
registers you read,

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:16.750
and that's what represents
voltage or current.

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:21.260
So the ingredient is the
configuration of this module.

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:24.610
Another ingredient would be if
I have that model I developed

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:26.950
that says when this
device is about to fail,

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:30.280
I run that in this analytic
module in the middle.

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:32.050
And that's converting
these data points

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:36.190
into real information
that's useful on the floor

00:27:36.190 --> 00:27:38.980
in the factory and can be
connected up to other systems,

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:41.710
like their maintenance
system or an in-line system,

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:44.210
to notify them of problems.

00:27:44.210 --> 00:27:46.660
So this brings me
back to Ellen's slide.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:50.410
And this is important
because if you

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:51.910
take a look at that
first piece, it

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:55.920
says we're going to
tackle bit-sized chunks.

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:58.740
If you modularize
this approach, you

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:01.860
can identify what components you
need within your infrastructure

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:05.670
to enable you to go solve that
particular business problem,

00:28:05.670 --> 00:28:08.160
and those are the
chunks you bite off.

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:10.310
In your solution recipe,
you have the ingredients.

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:11.168
You know the code.

00:28:11.168 --> 00:28:12.710
You know the
configurations that need

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:16.100
to be there that solve that
particular business problem.

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:19.050
But that's all you're chewing
at that point in time.

00:28:19.050 --> 00:28:21.320
So you're associating
development costs

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:23.480
and infrastructure costs
directly with the business

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:26.260
value that you're creating.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:29.020
It also helps you to identify
the value of some of the bigger

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:31.300
pieces within the
infrastructure, all

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:34.360
of these investments
in data warehousing.

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:36.640
Those are big things
that costs a lot of money

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:38.900
and take a lot of
time to set up.

00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:42.250
So being able to say that
I have a whole portfolio

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:46.360
of different use cases
I'm going to go support

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:47.980
and being able to
associate that back

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:50.980
to that investment
in the data warehouse

00:28:50.980 --> 00:28:55.890
helps you to justify that as
a larger scale deployment.

00:28:55.890 --> 00:28:58.620
And finally, we're focusing
on the business need.

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:00.280
This is all focused
on the solutions.

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:01.655
This is all focused
on the things

00:29:01.655 --> 00:29:04.860
that we're trying to generate in
terms of value for our clients,

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:06.600
and not on the specific
technology that's

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:08.158
underneath it.

00:29:08.158 --> 00:29:09.700
On one of the previous
slides, I said

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:13.570
industrial IoT is the
operating system for solutions.

00:29:13.570 --> 00:29:15.190
If I'm running and
working in Excel,

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:17.232
I don't necessarily care
if it's running on a Mac

00:29:17.232 --> 00:29:19.255
or if it's running
on a Windows machine.

00:29:19.255 --> 00:29:20.630
It's Excel that
I'm interested in

00:29:20.630 --> 00:29:23.320
and the work that I'm
able to do inside of it.

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:25.570
Industrial IoT
infrastructure needs

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:28.960
to provide the right
level of capabilities

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:30.670
and then disappear
into the background

00:29:30.670 --> 00:29:33.070
so that we can allow solutions
to go give the benefit

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:36.120
to the client that they need.

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:39.620
So this is how we
deliver solutions in IoT

00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:42.284
using building
blocks and solutions.

