00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:14.843
and control out of
it that we need.

00:36:14.843 --> 00:36:15.760
ELLEN DOWD: All right.

00:36:15.760 --> 00:36:18.620
And again, I'm going to let Rob
talk about some of the details

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:19.120
here.

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:21.640
But this customer, I think,
is particularly interesting,

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:23.920
because there is
probably nothing

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:27.970
more old school traditional
when it comes to manufacturing

00:36:27.970 --> 00:36:29.560
as steel manufacturing.

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:32.380
Steel manufacturing
is one of the first,

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:34.750
literally one of the first,
manufacturing operations

00:36:34.750 --> 00:36:36.880
that ever existed
anywhere in the world.

00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:40.880
And it hasn't really modernized
all that much over that time.

00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:43.900
So we're working with
a US steel manufacturer

00:36:43.900 --> 00:36:46.450
who is looking at solving--

00:36:46.450 --> 00:36:49.180
they're actually not
envisioning this grand new world

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:50.050
of the future.

00:36:50.050 --> 00:36:52.240
They have massive
operational challenges

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:54.910
that they need to address,
and they believe that,

00:36:54.910 --> 00:36:58.670
by looking at IoT, by looking
at better usage of their data,

00:36:58.670 --> 00:36:59.960
they can drive real results.

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:02.740
So they had any
number of problems

00:37:02.740 --> 00:37:07.790
around unplanned downtime
of their manufacturing line.

00:37:07.790 --> 00:37:09.680
A bunch of stuff
was done manually

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:11.720
and, therefore, not optimized.

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:14.440
They had mislabeled products
coming off the line.

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:16.360
They had any number
of challenges there.

00:37:16.360 --> 00:37:18.700
So we went in and
helped them, again,

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:20.992
prioritize those use cases
and decide what to go after.

00:37:20.992 --> 00:37:22.867
ROBERT LIVELY: And the
challenge here, again,

00:37:22.867 --> 00:37:25.930
was that when we looked at
these use cases, the problems

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:29.530
that they were finding,
that it required

00:37:29.530 --> 00:37:31.683
a vast array of different
kinds of sensors

00:37:31.683 --> 00:37:33.100
and different kind
of capabilities

00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:36.400
that we literally
developed with partners

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:38.390
that didn't exist before.

00:37:38.390 --> 00:37:39.710
So we were able to--

00:37:39.710 --> 00:37:41.950
for example, one
of the instances

00:37:41.950 --> 00:37:46.887
was looking at the
tension on a steel line

00:37:46.887 --> 00:37:48.470
and whether or not
there was potential

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:50.200
that steel line could break.

00:37:50.200 --> 00:37:52.600
So we actually worked with
the sensor manufacturers

00:37:52.600 --> 00:37:56.000
to design something
specifically for that use case.

00:37:56.000 --> 00:37:58.690
And then it starts spitting off
some of this interesting data.

00:37:58.690 --> 00:38:02.563
We needed to be able to easily,
rapidly deploy connectivity

00:38:02.563 --> 00:38:04.480
components out there
that would take advantage

00:38:04.480 --> 00:38:07.957
of that new information,
standardize it, bring it

00:38:07.957 --> 00:38:10.540
into the infrastructure so that
we could do something with it,

00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:12.650
and solve the business case.

00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:15.282
The other thing was that
in one particular instance,

00:38:15.282 --> 00:38:16.990
the client felt as
though they understood

00:38:16.990 --> 00:38:20.110
what the problem was with a
particular aspect of cutting

00:38:20.110 --> 00:38:21.460
some steel.

00:38:21.460 --> 00:38:23.740
And as we started to
put things in place

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:26.710
to observe what was going on,
we identified that that actually

00:38:26.710 --> 00:38:27.620
wasn't the case.

00:38:27.620 --> 00:38:30.850
It was something else
about that process that

00:38:30.850 --> 00:38:33.490
was causing the quality issues.

00:38:33.490 --> 00:38:34.900
And we were able
to quickly throw

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:37.630
in an additional
sensor, additional piece

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:40.630
of information, plug it into
the same infrastructure,

00:38:40.630 --> 00:38:43.330
stream that data up,
and immediately convert

00:38:43.330 --> 00:38:45.370
from solving one
aspect of that problem

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:47.300
to a different aspect
of that problem.

00:38:47.300 --> 00:38:52.060
So, once again, those components
and the approach of modularity

00:38:52.060 --> 00:38:54.850
helps us to respond
and adapt very quickly

00:38:54.850 --> 00:38:57.910
and deliver a business
value for the client.

00:38:57.910 --> 00:39:00.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

